rainian  border and  you don't have to actually you know  crawl in the forest in the dark to  provide uh food and water to  to children and people and  here you like we welcome them with the  open arms we are absolutely happy that  the polish government is is you know  welcoming so openly and we are welcoming  openly as well ukrainians but uh and  non-ukrainian uh you know citizens but  um  it is still appalling just yesterday um  we heard again about you know a group of  eight syrian people being pushed back to  belarus on the polish belarusian border  so this discrepancy this kind of  unexplainable you know uh uh  policy  is still there and is still in place and  uh the things on the polish belarusian  border are still happening  i i cannot expect i i don't know what's  the what's the idea behind this i think  uh it's it's it's i i could use the word  racism but i i don't know if it's racism  if it's just a you know eu policy  uh sort of  narrative for many years and not opening  the borders so much to  non-neighboring countries right  let's ask alberto about what he thinks  about this this discrepancy alberto how  do you explain this what should we make  of europe's schizophrenic uh refugee  response  well first of all i understand perfectly  the claim that what we are seeing now  are  double standards and indeed the  situation in poland is uh quite  illustrative of a trend that we have  seen for many years uh you mentioned  before the syrian crisis and the  inability of member states to reach  consensus on how to divide  responsibility and offer protection for  the many hundreds thousands of people  who were also running from a conflict i  i think that  possibly the geographical proximity to  the world itself may have  led to a significant change in  perception  in the european public but it is also  clear that  it also depends on european governments  and their response and how they frame  the emergency that determines  a change in sensitivity in the public at  large to go back to what monica was  saying i think we are seeing  clearly an overwhelming outpour of  solidarity from ordinary citizens but  it's also clear that this will not be  sustainably in the long term and so  member states and the eu  as they all will be called to its  responsibility to  offer protection to everybody in the  case of these conflict to ukrainians and  others who are running for their lives  from ukraine but it's also necessary to  offer protection without discrimination  on the basis of nationality or any other  factor and for these reasons it is  important and i hope that this will be a  watershed moment that will also  determine determine a change  of intent and and more courage on the  part of the eu uh to uh have a  consolidated common european asylum  system uh in the future  your thoughts about this europe says it  will give these refugees extended rights  to stay in the european union but these  protections from what i understand for  now will only apply to refugees who are  ukrainian citizens right so what happens  to all those who are from  third countries the third nationals  who've also tried to escape this  conflict what concessions should be  offered to them safety i mean that's  very important everyone who is running a  situation of war and and conflict has to  find a safety or you don't become  refugee by choice  and countries have legal and moral  obligations and that's why it's very  important that humanity cannot be  applied selectively uh saying that also  we have seen even in the other crisis uh  neighbors  have stepped forward uh hosting refugees  at the same scale i mean majority of  syrians majority of afghans uh majority  of those those who are fleeing conflict  in in africa and and the rohingyas they  have been taken care by uh the neighbor  that's why the word solidarity is very  important even in this context as well  and we see when when the civil society  when populations when the governments  everyone comes together  we can deal with humanitarian disasters  better and give some hope to the people  who are affected by our conflict and war  monica on solidarity is the polish  government are polish authorities  preparing for the presence of these  refugees in the long run you think  i don't think so i mean uh  first of all as it was said already it's  unprecedented so i i don't think any  government would be would be able to  handle it  well you know but uh  just over a week ago just when the when  the war started um the the polish  minister of you know internal affair  first came on tv and said we are very  well prepared we have been preparing for  many weeks which was absolutely not not  true at the time a lot of uh or majority  of the work which is now being done  i think it it stays on the shoulders of  local authorities non-governmental you  know groups initiatives organizations  i am seeing this every day i was also  talking uh to many representatives of  local authorities they quite complain  for the lack of you know support from  the government  and i don't think um that there is a  sort of a long-term thinking already in  play i would love to i would i would  love to hear about this if there is some  but i think at the moment um it's just  really sort of dealing with emergency  yeah speaking of dealing with the  emergency barbara there's been talk  there's been discussion of setting up a  humanitarian corridor that would allow  you know safe passage for the refugees  for the people who are still inside how  would that work i mean and would that  help you think  it's needed uh i mean first we need this  this war to end i mean as you heard uh  the unhigh commissioner for refugees  philippo grandi as far as bombs are  falling uh people have to run uh for for  safety and that's why we are very  concerned those people uh who need a  safe passage uh to to get out  it it has to be implemented right now  that's the need uh in in inside ukraine  and and then we can try to support on  humanitarian bases in terms of agencies  like unicr and others uh icrc and and  and others in in terms of uh uh kind of  making this possible logistically it is  possible but security wise we need the  guarantees from all sides to to to make  it happen and it should happen as soon  as possible security guarantees babar  what about financially money-wise what  what are the needs right now  we launched as as as the u.n and the  humanitarian larger community we  launched  like a week ago in a funding appeal for  1.7 billion us dollars for just the  short term uh  and this was for inside ukraine and also  for those countries that have been  receiving uh refugees uh the support has  been outpouring in terms of finances as  well there have been many contributions  we as unicr have received more than 200  million u.s dollars to support  refugees and also people inside but this  is just short term  more will be needed more support will be  needed it's also encouraging to see  not governments but individuals  businesses and foundations coming  forward to to contribute support is  coming but more is needed you say babar  alberto i'll give you the last word the  eu's migration policy looking ahead now  i mean there were proposals for uh  legislative reform uh to eu migration uh  and asylum law on the table even before  this ukraine conflict began do you see  this being hurried now and and do you  expect concrete steps  uh to be taken towards addressing the  gaps that have existed like i said this  is a historic moment and uh um to answer  your question i i believe that the  divisions uh and the sticking points of  the past will not be forgotten overnight  i think they will remain  divisions and and uh disagreements  between member states in the south south  and and northwest and east of of of  europe but what is clear uh based on  what we are seeing now is that there is  more that brings these countries  together then set them apart  so the question is in this context